  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM EDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nCurrently still cloudy across much of the northern/eastern Tennessee \nValley, but we are clearing out from west to east with lingering \nclouds from early morning convection. CU field out there is \ncurrently also a bit slow to develop this afternoon. Still think \nwe'll see some afternoon convection, but I'm a bit more pessimistic \nthan some of the previous days of model runs with these clouds \nsticking in. Mid and upper levels of the atmosphere should be \nconducive to producing thunderstorms that could bring gusty winds \nand isolated flooding from all the rain overnight. At this time \ncan't rule out a few strong to severe thunderstorms, but the threat \nfor widespread severe weather appears low.\n\nTomorrow we finally start to turn the corner into more fall-like \nweather (at least for a little while). The upper low associated with \nthe rex block, shifts southeast to the Carolinas region Wednesday \nnight into Thursday, allowing for more northerly flow to move into \nthe region, bringing in significantly cooler and drier air. Wednesday \nwon't be the real cool day, but it should help keep parts of \nsoutheast Tennessee below 90 degrees for the first time in quite a \nwhile.\n\nThis pleasant pattern persists through the weekend with temps \nprimarily in the 70's for most of the Valley. We'll begin to warm \nback up next week (back into the 80's for many) as ridging builds \nback in over the central/eastern United States. However with it \nturning more into fall, these strong ridges USUALLY don't set up for \nas prolonged of periods.&&",
